1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,010
Listen to a talk in an anthropology class.

2
00:00:04,010 --> 00:00:07,719
Previously, we discussed the significance of pottery to ancient cultures,

3
00:00:07,719 --> 00:00:12,170
including pottery as an art form and pottery's crucial role in trade.

4
00:00:12,170 --> 00:00:15,230
Today, we'll focus on the development of pottery over time.

5
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:22,170
One breakthrough was the invention of the potter's wheel around 3500 BCE in Mesopotamia.

6
00:00:22,170 --> 00:00:26,300
Before this, pottery was shaped mostly by hand

7
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:29,150
which was slow and produced less uniform results.

8
00:00:29,150 --> 00:00:32,100
The potter's wheel allowed artisans to spin

9
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:34,228
and shape the clay more quickly and evenly,

10
00:00:34,228 --> 00:00:38,290
making it possible to create symmetrical vessels with greater precision and speed.

11
00:00:38,290 --> 00:00:43,392
Firing technology also advanced. Early pots were fired in open pits,

12
00:00:43,392 --> 00:00:48,030
which limited temperature control, resulting in uneven or fragile pots.

13
00:00:48,030 --> 00:00:54,710
So kilns were developed: enclosed ovens that could reach higher and more consistent temperatures.

14
00:00:54,710 --> 00:00:58,050
These allowed for stronger, more durable pottery.

15
00:00:58,050 --> 00:01:00,740
Another advance was the updraft kiln

16
00:01:00,900 --> 00:01:03,170
which directed heat upward through the chamber.

17
00:01:03,170 --> 00:01:07,616
By adjusting the airflow, potters could control how much oxygen reached the fire.

18
00:01:07,616 --> 00:01:10,352
This helped them produce pottery with specific colors,

19
00:01:10,352 --> 00:01:15,140
like red or black, depending on how the clay reacted to the firing atmosphere.

20
00:01:15,140 --> 00:01:21,114
Then, in ancient China, potters developed kilns that could reach very high temperatures.

21
00:01:21,114 --> 00:01:25,250
This allowed them to create stoneware and eventually porcelain.

22
00:01:25,250 --> 00:01:28,680
Porcelain is made from a special mix of fine clay

23
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:34,070
and minerals and must be fired at temperatures above 1200°C.

24
00:01:34,070 --> 00:01:35,920
The result is a smooth

25
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:40,230
white ceramic that is strong, lightweight, and slightly translucent.

26
00:01:40,230 --> 00:01:43,940
Porcelain was a major breakthrough because it was more durable

27
00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:49,190
and versatile than earlier pottery.
